WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.640 --> 00:00:03.323
- So, what's the wave function?

00:00:03.323 --> 00:00:07.550
You said it's an interesting detail but

00:00:07.550 --> 00:00:09.760
in any interpretation, what is

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:11.900
the wave function in quantum mechanics?

00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:13.280
- Well, you know, we had this idea

00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:15.560
from Rutherford that atoms look like

00:00:15.560 --> 00:00:18.450
little solar systems,
but people very quickly

00:00:18.450 --> 00:00:20.560
realized that can't possibly be right

00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:23.260
because if an electron
is orbiting in a circle

00:00:23.260 --> 00:00:24.980
it will give off light, all the light

00:00:24.980 --> 00:00:26.200
that we have in this room comes from

00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:28.390
electrons zooming up and down, wiggling

00:00:28.390 --> 00:00:30.085
and that's what electromagnetic waves are

00:00:30.085 --> 00:00:32.450
and you can calculate
how long would it take

00:00:32.450 --> 00:00:34.830
for the electron just to
spiral into the nucleus

00:00:34.830 --> 00:00:37.470
and the answer is 10 to
the -11 seconds, okay?

00:00:37.470 --> 00:00:39.040
A hundred billionth of a second.

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:42.470
So that's not right, meanwhile

00:00:42.470 --> 00:00:44.420
people had realized that light

00:00:44.420 --> 00:00:47.104
which we understood from
the 1800s was a wave

00:00:47.104 --> 00:00:50.850
had properties that were
similar to that of particles.

00:00:50.850 --> 00:00:53.590
This is Einstein and
Plonk and stuff like that.

00:00:53.590 --> 00:00:56.180
So, if something that we agree was

00:00:56.180 --> 00:00:59.184
a wave had particle-like properties

00:00:59.184 --> 00:01:01.930
then maybe something
we think is a particle

00:01:01.930 --> 00:01:05.390
the electron has wave-like
properties, right?

00:01:05.390 --> 00:01:07.680
And so a bunch of people eventually

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:09.400
came to the conclusion don't think about

00:01:09.400 --> 00:01:11.690
the electron as a little point particle

00:01:11.690 --> 00:01:14.450
orbiting like a solar system, think of it

00:01:14.450 --> 00:01:16.840
as a wave that is spread out, and they

00:01:16.840 --> 00:01:18.960
cleverly gave this the
name the wave function

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:20.460
which is the dopiest name in the world

00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:22.350
for one of the most profound things

00:01:22.350 --> 00:01:26.400
in the universe, there's
literally a number

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:28.965
at every point in space,
which is the value

00:01:28.965 --> 00:01:31.205
of the electron's wave
function at that point.

00:01:31.205 --> 00:01:34.384
- And there's only, there's
only one wave function.

00:01:34.384 --> 00:01:36.460
- Yeah, they eventually figured that out.

00:01:36.460 --> 00:01:38.470
That took longer, but when you have

00:01:38.470 --> 00:01:40.590
two electrons, you do not have

00:01:40.590 --> 00:01:42.110
a wave function for electron one

00:01:42.110 --> 00:01:43.990
and a wave function for electron two.

00:01:43.990 --> 00:01:46.650
You have one combined wave function

00:01:46.650 --> 00:01:48.700
for both of them, and indeed, as you say.

00:01:48.700 --> 00:01:50.030
There's only one wave function

00:01:50.030 --> 00:01:51.793
for the entire universe at once.

